{
  "name": "Monthly analyst (A and A Reclass) charges to RCs",
  "today": "Corporate invoices Enron North America for all analyst program costs. Each month Financial Operations makes a manual journal entry charging $6,400 per analyst to each RC, using a headcount file taken from the Org Database. All of the roughly 63 Energy Operations managers are asked to find the \"A and A Reclass\" lines on their RC report every month and check the analyst count. When the Org Database is out of date (for example, an analyst has left), the RC is charged wrongly; the manager calls or emails, and Financial Operations has to process correcting journal entries before a deadline.",
  "change": "Post the analyst charges as an automatic monthly allocation from the Org Database and send each charged manager the names being charged, instead of a hand-keyed entry that every manager must hunt for on the RC report.",
  "explanation": "Financial Operations sets the analyst charge up as a recurring allocation (or scheduled upload) that reads the analyst assignments per RC from the Org Database, which the mail already names as the source of headcount. Before it posts each month, the job emails each manager whose RC is being charged a short list of the analysts' names and the amount, with a few days to reply. Managers charged for no analyst do nothing. A manager who sees a name that has left replies, the Org Database is fixed, and the charge is right when it posts, so no correcting journal entry is needed. Financial Operations only reviews the allocation instead of building the file and keying the entry.",
  "occurrences": [
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/836.eml",
      "quote": "Financial Operations makes a manual Journal Entry each month to charge the appropriate RC.  Please check your January 2000 RC report and make sure you were properly charged for the correct number of analysts you have currently."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/863.eml",
      "quote": "Please review the attached files for January and February to ensure that the proper RC was and is going to be charged.  If you have any corrections, please contact Brian Heinrich at Ext. 5-7677 ASAP since journal entries will need to be processed by this Friday, March 3rd."
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/836.eml",
      "quote": "Please continue to check your RC report each month going forward.",
      "note": "Every manager on the list (about 63) is asked to repeat the check every month, whether or not they have an analyst."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/863.eml",
      "quote": "I have received a few calls and E-mails stating that some reclasses need to be made because you no longer have an analyst in your group.",
      "note": "Errors are found only after posting and need correcting entries; a named list sent before posting catches a departed analyst before the charge is made."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/863.eml",
      "quote": "The information used by Financial Operations to charge your RC was obtained from the Org Database.",
      "note": "The charge already comes mechanically from the Org Database, so the hand-built file and manual entry can be replaced by an automatic allocation."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/863.eml",
      "quote": "This is the file we used for January, and the one for February.",
      "note": "Financial Operations builds a headcount file by hand each month for the entry."
    }
  ],
  "ongoing": true,
  "stated_rate": {
    "count": 1,
    "per": "month",
    "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/836.eml",
    "quote": "Financial Operations makes a manual Journal Entry each month to charge the appropriate RC."
  },
  "minutes": 210
}

{
